WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.050
This is a client pitch for an open-source whiteboard app.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.390
Two features, built as working code,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.750
to get new visitors to save their first drawing.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.070
Right now,

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.030
the empty canvas tells a new visitor that their drawing lives in the browser,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.130
and that they should save it to a file.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.970
But the moment they start drawing,

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.190
that message disappears, and nothing takes its place.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.490
Saving is tucked away in the main menu.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.170
So our team of four built two features against

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.430
one number: new visitors who save their first drawing.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.810
The first is a Save to file button that's always on screen.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.370
The second is a one-time prompt.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.070
It waits for a real shape, fifteen seconds of visible time,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.130
and two seconds of stillness, so it never interrupts a stroke.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.570
Screen readers announce it, and it never steals focus.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.750
Both of them open the same picker.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.170
The editable file is recommended, with PNG and SVG right beside it.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.030
Once you've saved, the prompt never comes back, but the button stays.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.450
Every colour pair meets the accessibility contrast standard,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.490
in light mode and in dark.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.770
And if you open the picker from the keyboard and close it,

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.890
focus goes right back to the button.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:20.690
I made twenty-two of the twenty-seven commits on main, across six tagged releases.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.870
The test is sized at three thousand one hundred thirty-five users per arm,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.530
but it never ran on real traffic, so there's no result to claim.
